# Round 93.  THIRTY-THREE files, written by the chief in one edit for the reason
# round 84 and round 86 both record: lanes do not write their own allowlist
# lines.  This is the same debt round 87 paid once and did not finish paying.
# Round 87 swept the hypothesis ledger's refs against check-ignore and added the
# one report it found.  Nothing swept the coverage matrix, because the check it
# added lives beside verify_hypothesis_ledger.py and reads the ledger only, so
# the matrix kept its own copy of the defect for six more days: thirty-three of
# the one hundred and twelve paths cited by docs/FUNCTIONAL_COVERAGE.json were
# invisible to version control, cited by seventeen capability rows across eight
# domains, and NINE of those rows are graded runtime_pass.  A fresh clone has
# never contained the evidence behind those nine claims, and running the
# coverage verifier on one exits 2 with a missing-reference error -- which is to
# say our own gate has not been reproducible from git on any machine, and the
# reason nobody noticed is that everybody runs it on the disk that wrote the
# files.  They are added rather than the references dropped, because the
# references are the part that is correct.  These are the Codex-era capture and
# reverse-engineering write-ups from 2026-08-13 to 2026-08-15, 240 KB of
# markdown in total, and they are the primary record of what the client was
# observed doing before this project started keeping a ledger.
# The test that found them is tests/test_functional_coverage.py, added in the
# same commit, and it is a test rather than a verifier guard for the same reason
# the ledger one is: it has to ask git, and the verifier stays runnable with the
# standard library and no repository.
